// ═══ §4 — RISKS ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

risks = {
  r1_substrate_stress_masks_real_incidents: "co-locating the SOC's own load-
    generation experiment with the SOC's job of detecting REAL incidents creates
    a confound: if n02 degrades during Stage A, is that the deliberate stressor
    working as designed, or a real problem the SOC should be flagging? Recommend
    tagging/dating the deliberate-stressor window explicitly so a future
    incident review can rule it in or out.",
  r2_es_resource_creep: "Elasticsearch is explicitly flagged as the heavy
    consumer (stack.engine) — if phase_1's minimal sizing is skipped or rushed,
    this is the most likely single cause of an actual n02 viability incident
    during Stage A. AC3 exists specifically to force real measurement before
    any scale-up.",
  r3_h0_confidence_gap: "per §0, H0 is 'operationally live, not battle-tested'.
    Stage A itself doesn't depend on H0, but if Stage A's own load turns out to
    be the FIRST real trigger of a genuine unprompted S4→S3→B actuation, that
    would be happening under exactly the conditions §0 flagged as unproven.
    Not a reason to block Stage A (Stage A's own risk is independent of this),
    but worth having rmw3 aware that Stage A could inadvertently BE the first
    real-world test of the production loop — treat any real actuation event
    during Stage A's burn-in as a significant, closely-watched moment, not
    routine background activity.",
  r4_vlan_lxc_sprawl: "this plan adds ≥1 new VLAN and ≥3 new services on n02,
    already flagged repeatedly this project as densely populated (7 LXCs +
    host). No capacity assessment was done in this pass — recommend a quick
    n02 headroom check (disk/RAM/CPU) before phase_0, given
    [[project_algedonic_storage_blindspot]]'s prior LVM-thin near-full incident.
    rmw3 2026-07-31 (aside, [[docs/spec/relay/lxc105_formal_study_2026-07-31.vsm]]
    thread): 'y luego con Owlh se suma mas infraestructura ;)' — flagging that
    the 3-VM Wazuh count is a FLOOR, not the ceiling. OwlH (Suricata/Zeek IDS
    management console, commonly paired with Wazuh for network-layer coverage
    alongside Wazuh's host-layer EDR) is a real, named candidate for a later
    addition to the same SOC VLAN — not scoped or sized in this pass, purely
    consigned here so r4's capacity-check recommendation accounts for expected
    future growth, not just Stage A's initial 3 VMs, when it's eventually run.",
}
